    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "As global climate patterns continue to change as a result of anthropogenic emissions, it is important to accurately predict how ecologically relevant variables, such as plant growth rates, respond to these changes. Plant growth is tied to the mycorrhizal fungi that grow on their roots, from whom they receive inorganic nutrients such as nitrogen in exchange for the organic products of photosynthesis. A global increase in plant biomass has been observed in response to rising atmospheric CO2 levels, presumably due to an increase in rates of photosynthesis. However, this fertilization effect seems highly dependent on the presence and accessibility of inorganic nutrients in the soil and appears to be mediated by plant-mycorrhizal interactions. This study investigates the effect of elevated atmospheric CO2 (eCO2) on nutrient exchange between the tree Pinus muricata and its mycorrhizal partner Suillus pungens. Pinus muricata seedlings colonized by S. pungens were grown for five months under either ambient or eCO2 conditions and were given access to variable amounts of nitrogen held within organic compounds. After this period, differences in seedling growth rates and nutrient levels were assessed using biomass metrics and isotopic analysis of foliar tissue. This study found no evidence of eCO2 fertilization under either organic nitrogen regime despite increased seedling photosynthesis under eCO2, potentially highlighting errors in assumptions made by the earth systems models (ESMs) that are used to predict rates of carbon sequestration by forest ecosystems. This study’s conclusions may provide a more accurate picture of how the world’s forests will respond to anthropogenic climate change and highlights important considerations to keep in mind when using ESMs to inform policy.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Cessna, A., Pelletier, P., and Peay, K. (2024). Forest Growth in a Changing World: Assessing the Impact of Elevated CO2 on Nitrogen Mineralization, Nutrient Exchange, and Plant Growth in Pinus muricata Seedlings Colonized by the Mycorrhizal Fungus Suillus pungens. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/jx250dc7735. https://doi.org/10.25740/jx250dc7735.",
